        "ExplanationCorrect": "The sales mix variance isolates the contribution-margin effect of selling products in proportions different from the budgeted mix, holding total unit volume constant and using standard (budgeted) contribution margins. Step 1 — Determine the budgeted mix percentages: Basic 5,000 / 10,000 = 50%, Deluxe 5,000 / 10,000 = 50%. Step 2 — Apply budgeted mix to actual total units (10,000): Basic = 50% x 10,000 = 5,000 budgeted-mix units, Deluxe = 5,000 budgeted-mix units. Step 3 — Compute the mix variance for each product using standard CMs: Basic = (Actual 4,200 - Budgeted-mix 5,000) x $30 = -800 x $30 = -$24,000 (unfavorable, sold fewer low-CM units). Deluxe = (Actual 5,800 - Budgeted-mix 5,000) x $55 = +800 x $55 = +$44,000 (favorable, sold more high-CM units). Step 4 — Total sales mix variance = -$24,000 + $44,000 = $20,000 favorable. The variance is favorable because the actual mix shifted toward the higher-CM Deluxe product. A common exam trap is to use actual CMs instead of standard CMs or to compute only one product's mix effect while ignoring the offsetting shift in the other product.",
