        "SectionName": "Performance Management",
        "Topic": "C.075 cost of quality maturity assessment",
        "MicroTopic": "COQ maturity TQM assessment",
        "UniqueConceptKey": "C-D075-coq-maturity-tqm-assessment",
        "LOSTag": "C Performance management",
        "Difficulty": "Moderate",
        "ItemType": "MCQ",
        "ItemStyle": "single-select",
        "Stem": "Oakhaven Manufacturing's annual cost of quality report shows the following: Prevention costs $380,000 (supplier audits, design reviews, process capability studies, quality training), Appraisal costs $120,000 (in-process inspection, testing, calibration), Internal failure costs $45,000 (scrap and rework caught before shipment), and External failure costs $22,000 (warranty claims and customer returns). Total quality costs are $567,000. The controller is presenting this COQ profile to the board as evidence of the company's quality management maturity. How should this COQ profile be characterized?",
        "Choices": {
            "A": "Early-stage quality control with heavy reliance on end-of-line inspection to catch defects, as indicated by the $120,000 in appraisal costs and the persistence of some internal failure costs.",
            "B": "A developing quality management system transitioning from detection to prevention, evidenced by the large prevention budget of $380,000 but still carrying $67,000 in combined failure costs.",
            "C": "A mature TQM organization with a well-developed prevention infrastructure, where dominant prevention spending (67.0% of total COQ) has systematically driven down failure costs to only 11.8% of total quality costs.",
            "D": "A Lean manufacturing operation focused on waste elimination and just-in-time production, where low failure costs result from built-in quality practices such as jidoka and poka-yoke rather than from conventional quality cost management."
        },
        "CorrectChoice": "C",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "The COQ profile reveals a mature quality management system. Total quality costs = $380,000 + $120,000 + $45,000 + $22,000 = $567,000. Conformance costs (Prevention + Appraisal) = $380,000 + $120,000 = $500,000 (88.2% of total). Non-conformance costs (Internal + External failure) = $45,000 + $22,000 = $67,000 (11.8% of total). The defining characteristic of a mature TQM organization is that prevention costs dominate the COQ profile because the organization invests heavily upstream — in supplier quality, design reviews, employee training, and process capability — which systematically prevents defects from occurring. As prevention investment increases, appraisal and failure costs decline. Here, prevention alone accounts for 67.0% of total COQ ($380,000 / $567,000), appraisal is moderate at 21.2%, and failure costs are contained at 11.8%. This is the inverse of the traditional quality-control profile (high failure, low prevention). The ratio of prevention to failure costs is 5.7:1 ($380,000 / $67,000), indicating a prevention-centric quality strategy that has achieved very low defect rates. An exam trap is to focus on the absolute dollar amount of appraisal costs ($120,000) without assessing it in the context of the total COQ distribution, which clearly shows prevention dominance.",
