        "CorrectChoice": "A",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the high-low method, the variable cost per unit = (Cost at high activity - Cost at low activity) / (High activity level - Low activity level). Using April as the high point and March as the low point: Variable cost per hour = ($17,400 - $13,500) / (1,600 - 1,100) = $3,900 / 500 = $7.80 per machine hour. Fixed cost = Total cost at high - (Variable rate x High activity) = $17,400 - ($7.80 x 1,600) = $17,400 - $12,480 = $4,920. The cost equation is Y = $4,920 + $7.80X. A common error is to divide a single month's total cost by its machine hours, which produces an average cost that includes both fixed and variable components rather than the incremental variable rate.",
