        "Stem": "Oakmont Manufacturing migrated its ERP system from a legacy platform to a cloud-based solution during Q1 2026. Because the new ERP's three-way matching module required configuration that the implementation team estimated would take 12 weeks, management made a formal decision to disable the automated three-way matching control. As a compensating control, the accounts payable manager, Sandra Cole, was instructed to manually review each vendor invoice against the corresponding purchase order and receiving report before approving payment. Cole initialed each invoice package after completing her review and maintained a spreadsheet log of approved payments. In July 2026, an internal audit revealed that a $180,000 vendor invoice for raw materials was paid twice — once in April and again in June — because the vendor sent a duplicate invoice after the first payment was not recorded in the new ERP's payment history during the migration cutover. Cole stated she 'remembered reviewing both invoices but could not recall whether the April invoice had already been paid when she approved the June duplicate.' The spreadsheet log did not include any payment-tracking fields. The controller must evaluate whether the compensating manual control provided adequate coverage under COSO Principle 12. Which statement represents the most accurate analysis?",
