        "ItemType": "MCQ",
        "ItemStyle": "single-select",
        "Stem": "Pacific Rim Credit Union, a $4.1 billion financial institution, launched a mobile banking application that uses artificial intelligence to provide personalized financial advice to its 380,000 members. The AI model was developed by a third-party fintech vendor and was trained on aggregated member transaction data, credit scores, and demographic information. Six months after launch, the state banking regulator issued a cease-and-desist order, finding that the AI model systematically recommended higher-interest loan products to members in lower-income zip codes and investment products with higher fees to members over age 65, potentially violating fair lending and elder financial protection laws. Pacific Rim's risk register, last updated before the mobile app launch, did not include any risk related to AI model bias or algorithmic fairness. The Chief Risk Officer stated that 'model risk' was identified as a general category, but no specific AI-related risks had been assessed. Under COSO Principle 6, which of the following best evaluates Pacific Rim's risk identification?",
        "Choices": {
            "A": "Pacific Rim adequately identified the risk because 'model risk' was included as a general category in the risk register, and COSO Principle 6 requires risk identification at the category level rather than at the level of specific technologies or algorithms.",
            "B": "Pacific Rim failed to identify a material risk arising from a significant change in its business operations — deploying an AI-driven advisory tool without assessing the specific risks of algorithmic bias, regulatory compliance, and fair lending represents a gap in risk identification that COSO Principle 6 requires organizations to address.",
            "C": "The regulatory order demonstrates that the risk identification was adequate because external regulators identified the issue — COSO Principle 6 recognizes that some risks can only be identified by external parties with specialized expertise.",
            "D": "The risk identification failure was the fintech vendor's responsibility — Pacific Rim reasonably relied on the vendor's representations that the AI model complied with applicable regulations, and COSO Principle 6 places the burden of risk identification on the party that develops the technology."
        },
        "CorrectChoice": "B",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 6 requires the organization to identify risks to the achievement of its objectives and analyze risks as a basis for determining how they should be managed. This includes identifying risks arising from significant changes in the business — regulatory, economic, and physical operating environment. Deploying an AI-powered advisory tool that makes loan and investment product recommendations to 380,000 members is a significant operational change that introduced specific, foreseeable risks: algorithmic bias, fair lending violations, elder financial protection regulations, and model governance requirements. Pacific Rim's risk register categorized 'model risk' generically without assessing the specific manifestations of model risk introduced by AI-driven advisory services — bias, explainability, regulatory compliance, data privacy, and third-party model governance. The regulatory order was not evidence that external identification compensated for internal failure — it was evidence that the risk manifesting was both material and foreseeable. In practice, organizations deploying AI in regulated industries should assess risks at the specific technology and use-case level, not rely on generic risk categories that predate the technology deployment.",
